11. NEUTRAL CONCLUSION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Under the stated baseline assumptions (6 tenants, ~360 runs/month,
~45 mins/run), the four options differ primarily in billing model,
operational overhead, and isolation posture:

- Fargate-Only (~$260–$325/month): Best for bursty/unpredictable
  workloads; lowest infra ops; cost scales linearly with usage.

- Multitenant ECS/EC2 (~$95–$110/month): Best unit economics at scale
  (9+ tenants); requires highest infra investment; shared host risk.

- Single-tenant ECS/EC2 (~$150–$220/month): Strongest isolation without
  shared host risk; cost scales linearly per tenant; no economy of scale.

- Cosmos dbt + Astronomer (~$200–$400/month): Highest per-run cost at
  current volume; lowest operational overhead; introduces vendor
  dependency; best for rapid time-to-market.

Recommendation framing: No single option dominates across all dimensions.
The preferred architecture depends on the organisation's priorities across
cost, isolation, operational capacity, and growth trajectory. A hybrid
model may be optimal as tenant count grows beyond 10.

This document is intended as a neutral input to the architecture and
finance discussion. Final decisions should incorporate non-compute costs,
regulatory requirements, and team capability assessments.

